msgid ""
"Active mode nudges your mouse cursor one pixel every minute or so (not a "
"specific timing). This setting can be used to keep your computer from "
"going to sleep. You might turn this setting off if you notice desktop "
"apps are preventing you from getting mobile notifications (for example on"
" Discord or Microsoft Teams)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:340
msgid "Chording"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:342
msgid ""
"CCOS devices feature the ability to :doc:`chord<Chords>`. The following "
"settings affect the device's chording abilities."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:345
msgid "Character Only Mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:347
msgid "``Path: GTM > Chording > Character Only Mode``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:349
msgid ""
"This setting is a toggle that disables chording capabilities on CCOS "
"devices. It is off by default and can be enabled in case we don't want "
"any chording at all. This setting can be useful in cases where we don't "
"want to accidentally trigger chords unintentionally, such as when gaming."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:351
msgid ""
"If your CCOS device suddenly loses its chording ability, it's a good idea"
" to check if this setting is toggled off."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:354
msgid "Press Tolerance"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:356
msgid "``Path: GTM > Chording > Press Tolerance``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:358
msgid ""
"The press tolerance refers to a window of time in which a chord can be "
"performed, measured in milliseconds (ms). This timer is initiated upon "
"the first \"press\" action of the first key in a chord and ends once the "
"last key of the chord is pressed, or until the press tolerance runs out, "
"whichever comes first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:-1
msgid "Diagram Explaining Tolerances"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:366
msgid ""
"Put simply, increasing the press tolerance (usually, done in conjunction "
"with increasing the :ref:`release tolerance <GenerativeTextMenu:Release "
"Tolerance>`) makes it easier to perform chords."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:369 ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:398
msgid ""
"The press tolerance scales (increases) according to the number of keys in"
" a chord. The window of time will be bigger with a 6-key chord than with "
"a 3-key chord. This means that, though you might set the tolerance to a "
"specific timing, it will actually be longer than that depending on how "
"many keys are in your chord."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:371 ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:400
msgid ""
"You can increase this setting in order to make that window of time longer"
" and make chording easier."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:373
msgid ""
"The downside to having higher values is that you may accidentally trigger"
" chords during normal character entry. Therefore, if you are noticing "
"chords fire unintentionally, it is a good idea to lower this setting "
"along with the :ref:`release tolerance <GenerativeTextMenu:Release "
"Tolerance>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:1
msgid "25 ms"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:1
msgid "150 ms"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:385
msgid "Release Tolerance"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:387
msgid "``Path: GTM > Chording > Release Tolerance``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:389
msgid ""
"The release tolerance refers to a window of time in which a chord can be "
"performed, measured in milliseconds (ms). This timer is initiated upon "
"the first \"release\" action of any key in a chord and ends once the "
"chord is fully performed, or until the release tolerance runs out, "
"whichever comes first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:395
msgid ""
"Put simply, increasing the release tolerance (usually, done in "
"conjunction with increasing the :ref:`press tolerance "
"<GenerativeTextMenu:Press Tolerance>`) makes it easier to perform chords."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:402
msgid ""
"The downside to having higher values is that you may accidentally trigger"
" chords during normal character entry. Therefore, if you are noticing "
"chords fire unintentionally, it is a good idea to lower this setting "
"along with the :ref:`press tolerance <GenerativeTextMenu:Press "
"Tolerance>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:1
msgid "18 ms"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:415
msgid "Timeout"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:417
msgid "``Path: GTM > Chording > Timeout``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:419
msgid ""
"This setting will change how long CCOS counts time in order to replace "
"characters that precede a chord."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:421
msgid ""
"CCOS devices have a running timer that starts after every single "
"character that is entered in traditional chentry (character entry, i.e. "
"one letter at a time). This timer controls whether or not the next chord "
"that you perform deletes the preceding characters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:423
msgid ""
"This feature allows users to misfire chords, yet be able to correct them "
"by quickly performing the chord correctly, without having to backspace "
"manually to erase the misfired chord. The result is that the timeout will"
" automatically backspace all of the preceding characters (up to the last "
":doc:`breaking character </Breaking Character>`) and replace them with "
"the intended chord."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:425
msgid ""
"You can set this setting to be as low as 0.0 seconds (s) or as high as "
"25.0 seconds (s), being able to adapt it to your individual "
":doc:`chording<Chords>` style."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:428
msgid ""
"Setting this setting to 0.0 s will result in :doc:`chords<Chords>` not "
"working at all. They will fire, but they will not automatically erase the"
" :ref:`chord inputs<Chord Input>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:1
msgid "4 s"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:1
msgid "0 s"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:1
msgid "25 s"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:1
msgid "0.1 s"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:440
msgid "Spurring"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:442
msgid "``Path: GTM > Chording > Spurring``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:444
msgid ""
"A 'chording only' mode which tells your device to output chords on a "
"press event rather than a press & release and release event. When in "
"spurring mode, you can press the keys of a chord one at a time with a "
"much longer waiting period, which makes it a useful mode for those who "
"want to practice chording without worrying about proper "
":ref:`timing<GenerativeTextMenu:Press Tolerance>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:446
msgid ""
"Spurring mode also enables you to jump from one chord to another without "
"releasing everything. It can provide significant speed gains when "
"chording, but also takes away the flexibility of character entry. "
"Spurring mode can truly maximize speed when chording if a user has chords"
" for all of the words they want to use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:449
msgid "Spurring On/Off"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:451
msgid "``Path: GTM > Chording > Character Only Mode > Spurring Timeout``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:453
msgid "This setting will toggle spurring mode ON or OFF."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:456
msgid "Spurring Timeout"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:458
msgid "``Path: GTM > Chording > Spurring > Spurring Timeout``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:460
msgid ""
"The time of inactivity to default back to fluid chorded/character entry "
"mode (aka spurring off)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:1
msgid "240 s"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:1
msgid "250 s"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:1
msgid "1 s"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:472
msgid "Arpeggiate"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:474
msgid "``Path: GTM > Chording > Arpeggiate``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:476
msgid ""
"Arpeggiate actions are timed actions that can modify a chord after the "
"chord is performed. A quick example of this is the use of chord modifiers"
" after you perform the chord. You can read that section for information "
"on how the :doc:`chord modifiers<Chord Modifiers>` work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:478
msgid ""
"With arpeggiates enabled, you can chord the word ``run`` and then, within"
" the :ref:`arpeggiate timeout window<GenerativeTextMenu:Arpeggiate "
"Timeout>`, press the past tense modifier for the word to be \"modified\" "
"into its past tense variant; in english, ``ran``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:482
msgid "Arpeggiate On/Off"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:484
msgid "``Path: GTM > Chording > Arpeggiate > Arpeggiate On/Off``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:486
msgid "This setting will let you toggle the arpeggiate capability ON or OFF."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:488
msgid ""
"Some users dislike arpeggiates as, in really fast typing, it may cause "
"unwanted modifications."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:491
msgid "Arpeggiate Timeout"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:493
msgid "``Path: GTM > Chording > Arpeggiate > Arpeggiate Timeout``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:495
msgid ""
"The arpeggiate timeout is a window of time after a chord is performed "
"during which CCOS will expect arpeggiates to be performed. After this "
"timer runs out, CCOS will NO LONGER modify the preceding chord."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:497
msgid ""
"A common issue that users may run into while having arpeggiates enabled "
"is the shift key modifying the preceding chord instead of the next key. "
"For this reason, some users lower the arpeggiate timeout to a really low "
"amount of time in order to reduce the possibility of this happening "
"unintentionally."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:500
msgid "Display"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:502
msgid ""
"Under this section, you'll find settings that deal with how your "
"CharaChorder displays certain things."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:505
msgid "Version"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:507
msgid "``Path: GTM > Display > Version``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:509
msgid ""
"Though this is not a setting that can be modified, it's a useful piece of"
" text that will show you the CCOS version that your CharaChorder is "
"currently on. You can use this to quickly check what version you are "
"running on the fly, anywhere that you can read GTM"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:512
msgid "Realtime Feedback"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:514
msgid "``Path: GTM > Display > Realtime Feedback``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:516
msgid "This setting toggles realtime feedback ON or OFF."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:518
msgid ""
"Realtime feedback refers to the helpful text like ``SPURRING_ON``, "
"``SPURRING_OFF`` etc, that lets the user know if a certain mode has been "
"activated or deactivated on the CharaChorder device. Since there is no "
"other visual way to know if the chord used to enable or disable certain "
"settings, it is helpful to have these texts pop up as confirmation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:521
msgid ""
"The realtime feedback setting controls the "
":ref:`startup<GenerativeTextMenu:Startup>` setting. If realtime feedback "
"is OFF, then startup will be OFF, regardless of that setting's individual"
" toggle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:524
msgid "Startup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:526
msgid "``Path: GTM > Display > Startup``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:528
msgid ""
"Having this setting set to \"ON\" will result in your device displaying "
"the text \"CCOS is ready.\" after you have plugged your device into a "
"computer. The startup text will be displayed wherever your cursor was "
"last typing, if that window is active. This setting is useful so that you"
" know when your CharaChorder device is ready to be used, since startup "
"may take a couple of seconds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:530
msgid ""
"However, if you have editable text highlighted when you connect your "
"CharaChorder, this setting will replace that highlighted text with the "
"startup message. Additionally, if there is not a selected window where "
"text may be typed, the key codes that the CharaChorder sends to your "
"computer in order to display the startup message may be interpreted by "
"your computer as actions and may result in unwanted behavior."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:532
msgid ""
"If you would rather not have this message display every time that you "
"connect your device, then you can toggle this setting OFF."
msgstr ""

#: ../../GenerativeTextMenu.rst:535
msgid ""
"The Startup setting is dependent on the :ref:`realtime feedback "
"setting<GenerativeTextMenu:Realtime Feedback>`. If that setting is set to"
" OFF, then Startup won't display, even if Startup is set to ON."
msgstr ""

